      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the exponent of 3 in the prime factorization of \\(24!\\), we need to determine how many times 3 appears as a factor in the numbers from 1 to 24. This can be done using the formula for the exponent of a prime \\(p\\) in \\(n!\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{exponent of } p \\text{ in } n! = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{p} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{p^2} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{p^3} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots\n\\]\n\nFor \\(p = 3\\) and \\(n = 24\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\text{exponent of } 3 \\text{ in } 24! = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{3} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{9} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{24}{27} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots\n\\]\n\nLet's compute this step-by-step using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Function to calculate the exponent of a prime p in n!\ndef exponent_in_factorial(n, p):\n    exponent = 0\n    power = p\n    while power <= n:\n        exponent += n // power\n        power *= p\n    return exponent\n\n# Calculate the exponent of 3 in 24!\nn = 24\np = 3\nexponent = exponent_in_factorial(n, p)\nprint(exponent)\n```\n```output\n10\n```\nThe exponent of 3 in the prime factorization of \\(24!\\) is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
